[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise. They were
merged by subject line ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)), so nobody read the
bodies. Read by tenant, form and what the body says, they split into three problems with three
different fixes.

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser fix queued for #4471 closes only the first row. The other two stay open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

## The three problems

- **`parser`: day and month swap.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant
  in #4471 writes the day first, so "7 April" goes out as "4 July". The fix queued for #4471.
- **`hint`: the form tells people to type it wrong.** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date
  field, people type month first, and the form reads the value day first. Only form reports from
  booking-v3 tenants. Fixing the parser does not touch it: the hint and the form's read have to change.
- **`one-day`: the date moves by a day.** Not a swap. "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in
  Toronto got Monday." The desk already listed these as `oneDayMove` in desk-notes; they belong to
  time-zone handling, not to the parser.

## How this was counted

- Every number on this page is computed at render time by `#lib/kb/split` from the ticket export
  and the tenant directory. Duplicate export rows count once (`uniqueById`).
- The cause comes from the tenant: its locale and its booking form (`causeOf` in `#lib/kb/causes`).
  Intake's `intakeTag` is a guess from the reporter's browser and is not used.
- `one-day` comes from the desk's own `oneDayMove` list; a full read of the bodies found no others.

## For the desk

- Do not close #4471 when the parser fix ships. Split it into the three tickets above.
- The same subject lines ("wrong day on the invite", "date is wrong", …) appear in all three problems. Read the body before you merge.

